for activity-blogs.js, the goal is not to build the blog system. It is only the bridge between your Markdown blog files and the Activity Center.

1. What the module does

The module's job is:

blog Markdown files
        ↓
discover their locations
        ↓
fetch each Markdown file
        ↓
extract front matter
        ↓
create normalized blog objects
        ↓
sort by publication date
        ↓
take recent posts
        ↓
render them in Activity Center

For example, suppose you have:

blog/
├── zombie-internet/
│   ├── post.md
│   └── cover.webp
│
├── ai-agents/
│   ├── post.md
│   └── cover.webp
│
└── rust-cli/
    ├── post.md
    └── cover.webp

And zombie-internet/post.md contains:

---
title: "Zombie Internet"
date: "2026-09-02"
description: "How mass AI-generated content is changing the internet."
cover: "cover.webp"
---

# Zombie Internet

Article content...

The activity module should ultimately turn that into something conceptually like:

{
    title: "Zombie Internet",
    date: "2026-09-02",
    description: "How mass AI-generated content is changing the internet.",
    cover: "blog/zombie-internet/cover.webp",
    url: "blog/zombie-internet/"
}

The Activity Center then decides how that object looks visually.

2. The important browser limitation

This is the one thing you need to account for before coding.

A browser cannot generally say:

const files = getEverythingInside("./blog/");

A static website doesn't expose directory contents to JavaScript.

So activity-blogs.js needs some source that tells it which Markdown files exist.

For the architecture we're building, I'd use a generated manifest:

blog/
├── zombie-internet/
│   └── post.md
├── ai-agents/
│   └── post.md
└── rust-cli/
    └── post.md

blog-index.json

The generated blog-index.json could contain:

[
    "blog/zombie-internet/post.md",
    "blog/ai-agents/post.md",
    "blog/rust-cli/post.md"
]

Then the browser can fetch that JSON.

This is not a second source of truth.

The Markdown files remain the source of truth.

The index is merely:

“Here are the Markdown files that currently exist.”

3. Initialization

Expose one function:

initBlogActivity()

main.js calls it when the website starts.

Something conceptually like:

main.js
   ↓
initBlogActivity()
   ↓
find #blog-activity
   ↓
load posts

If the Activity Center doesn't exist on the page, the module should simply return without exploding.

4. Configuration

Keep the configuration tiny.

Something along the lines of:

const BLOG_INDEX = '/blog-index.json';
const MAX_POSTS = 3;
const BLOG_ACTIVITY_CONTAINER = '#blog-activity';

You don't need twenty configuration constants.

The module isn't a framework. 😂

5. Fetch the blog index

First:

GET /blog-index.json

You expect:

[
    "blog/zombie-internet/post.md",
    "blog/ai-agents/post.md",
    "blog/rust-cli/post.md"
]

Validate that the response is actually an array.

If it isn't:

ERROR

If it's an empty array:

EMPTY

Otherwise continue.

6. Fetch every Markdown file

For every path in the index:

blog/zombie-internet/post.md
blog/ai-agents/post.md
blog/rust-cli/post.md

perform:

GET /blog/zombie-internet/post.md
GET /blog/ai-agents/post.md
GET /blog/rust-cli/post.md

You can fetch them concurrently with Promise.all().

Conceptually:

index
 ├── post.md ──→ fetch
 ├── post.md ──→ fetch
 └── post.md ──→ fetch
                    ↓
               Markdown strings
7. Parse the front matter

You don't need to parse the actual Markdown body for the Activity Center.

You only care about the metadata at the top.

Given:

---
title: "Zombie Internet"
date: "2026-09-02"
description: "How mass AI-generated content is changing the internet."
cover: "cover.webp"
---

# Zombie Internet

A bunch of article content...

you extract:

title
date
description
cover

The front matter is the section between:

---

and:

---

So conceptually:

Markdown
│
├── front matter
│   ├── title
│   ├── date
│   ├── description
│   └── cover
│
└── article body

The article body can be ignored by this module.

8. Don't build a giant Markdown parser

For this module, you don't need to interpret arbitrary Markdown.

You're only reading a very controlled front-matter format that you define.

You can parse it yourself.

For example:

---
title: "Zombie Internet"
date: "2026-09-02"
description: "Something..."
cover: "cover.webp"
---

Split the front matter into lines.

For each line:

key: value

extract:

key
value

Then remove surrounding quotes where appropriate.

That's enough for your current system.

If eventually you want full YAML support, that's when you can introduce a YAML parser.

9. Resolve the cover image path

This is an easy thing to accidentally screw up.

Suppose:

Markdown:
blog/zombie-internet/post.md

cover:
cover.webp

The actual image is:

blog/zombie-internet/cover.webp

So don't interpret:

cover.webp

as:

/cover.webp

Instead, resolve it relative to the Markdown file's directory.

Conceptually:

post.md
│
├── directory = blog/zombie-internet/
│
└── cover = cover.webp
             ↓
       blog/zombie-internet/cover.webp
10. Generate the blog URL

You also need a URL for clicking the activity item.

If your blog structure is:

blog/
└── zombie-internet/
    └── post.md

then you need to decide what the actual article URL is.

For example:

/blog/zombie-internet/

or:

/blog/zombie-internet.html

or perhaps:

/blog.html?post=zombie-internet

This part needs to match whatever blog-page architecture you eventually implement.

The Activity module shouldn't invent a routing system.

I'd recommend deciding this before you code the renderer.

11. Normalize the data

This is important because it keeps rendering independent from Markdown structure.

Every successful post should become:

{
    title,
    date,
    description,
    cover,
    url
}

So regardless of how the Markdown file looks internally, the Activity Center receives a predictable object.

For example:

{
    title: "Zombie Internet",
    date: "2026-09-02",
    description: "How mass AI-generated content is changing the internet.",
    cover: "/blog/zombie-internet/cover.webp",
    url: "/blog/zombie-internet/"
}
12. Validate posts

Don't blindly render garbage.

At minimum, require:

title
date

If a Markdown file has:

---
description: "blah"
---

but no title/date, skip that post.

You could optionally allow missing:

description
cover

because those aren't necessarily essential.

So:

title      REQUIRED
date       REQUIRED
description OPTIONAL
cover      OPTIONAL
13. Sort by date

Once you've loaded all posts:

post A → Sep 2
post B → Aug 28
post C → Sep 5
post D → Aug 10

sort:

Sep 5
Sep 2
Aug 28
Aug 10

Newest first.

Then:

posts.slice(0, MAX_POSTS)

If MAX_POSTS = 3, only those three reach the Activity Center.

14. What the Activity Center should display

Based on the design we established, the Activity Center isn't the full blog.

It's a recent activity feed.

Something like:

BLOG
────────────────────────────────

Zombie Internet
How mass AI-generated content is...
Sep 2, 2026

Building AI Agents
...
Sep 1, 2026

Why I Started Using Rust
...
Aug 28, 2026

Each item should be clickable.

Click:

Zombie Internet

→ opens that blog post.

The actual horizontally scrolling blog cards are handled by:

blog-horizontal-scroll.js

Not this module.

That's an important separation.

15. Rendering rules

Just like activity-github.js, use DOM APIs.

Don't do:

container.innerHTML = markdownData;

Especially because you're processing external file contents.

Instead:

createElement()
textContent
appendChild()

For example:

article
 ├── title link
 ├── description
 └── date

The title should be:

element.textContent = post.title;

rather than interpolated HTML.

16. Loading state

When initialization begins:

Loading blog activity...

Something visually consistent with GitHub Activity:

Fetching recent blog posts...

Then replace it when loading finishes.

17. Empty state

There are two possible empty conditions:

blog-index.json exists
        ↓
[]

or:

index has files
        ↓
all files invalid

Then display something like:

No recent blog posts.

Don't treat that as an application crash.

18. Error state

Actual infrastructure failure:

blog-index.json couldn't be fetched

or:

Markdown file couldn't be fetched

should produce:

Blog activity couldn't be loaded.

[Retry]

The Retry button simply invokes the loading process again.

19. What happens if ONE Markdown file fails?

I'd recommend partial failure, rather than killing the entire feed.

Example:

index:
A
B
C
D

A → success
B → success
C → 404
D → success

Result:

A
B
D

and silently skip C.

But if:

blog-index.json

itself fails, that's a genuine module-level failure.

So:

Index failure → ERROR

Individual post failure → skip that post

That's a much nicer user experience.

20. Final module architecture

The whole thing should mentally look like this:

                 blog-index.json
                       │
                       ▼
                ┌──────────────┐
                │ Fetch index  │
                └──────┬───────┘
                       │
                       ▼
              Markdown file paths
                       │
             ┌─────────┼─────────┐
             ▼         ▼         ▼
           post.md   post.md   post.md
             │         │         │
             └─────────┼─────────┘
                       ▼
                 Parse metadata
                       │
                       ▼
                Normalize objects
                       │
                       ▼
                  Validate posts
                       │
                       ▼
                  Sort by date
                       │
                       ▼
                  Limit results
                       │
                       ▼
                Render Activity

And the responsibility boundary is:

activity-blogs.js
        │
        ├── Fetch
        ├── Parse
        ├── Validate
        ├── Normalize
        ├── Sort
        └── Render

while:

blog-horizontal-scroll.js
        │
        └── Blog browsing interaction

and eventually your blog-page code handles:

Markdown → full article HTML
One architectural decision you need to make now

Before writing activity-blogs.js, decide the article URL format.

I'd personally go with:

/blog/<slug>/

where:

blog/
└── zombie-internet/
    └── post.md

maps to:

/blog/zombie-internet/

Then your normalized object is beautifully simple:

{
    title: "...",
    date: "...",
    description: "...",
    cover: "...",
    url: "/blog/zombie-internet/"
}




review on generated activity-github.js  :


Syntactically, this looks valid JavaScript. I don't see a syntax-level problem in the uploaded file.

But logically/architecturally: this is still the old Hygraph implementation. That's the big problem.

Your current file is explicitly built around Hygraph:

const DEFAULT_HYGRAPH_ENDPOINT = 'https://ap-south-1.cdn.hygraph.com/...';
const DEFAULT_AUTHOR_ID = 'cmrozug811d9n07pi8f11cm7y';

and the fetching pipeline sends GraphQL requests to it.

But what we just decided is:

Markdown files
      ↓
discover/index
      ↓
fetch .md
      ↓
parse front matter
      ↓
normalize
      ↓
sort
      ↓
Activity Center

So I would not debug this version further. It's solving the wrong problem.

There are also several pieces that are now unnecessary for the Markdown architecture:

DEFAULT_HYGRAPH_ENDPOINT
DEFAULT_AUTHOR_ID
GRAPHQL_QUERIES
customFetcher
GraphQL response handling
authorId
endpoint
readTime unless you specifically want it in the activity feed
tags unless you specifically want them there
the whole author-filtering fallback system

For example, this entire thing:

try {
    // query Hygraph by author
}

followed by:

if (!fetchSucceeded || rawPosts.length === 0) {
    // query recent posts
}

is leftover architecture we explicitly decided to get rid of.

There are a couple of smaller issues too.

MAX_POSTS uses ||:

this.maxPosts = options.maxPosts || MAX_POSTS;

so 0 can't be intentionally supplied. Same minor issue we found in activity-github.js.

Also, the module currently says it will display articles, excerpts, reading time, tags, etc. That's considerably more elaborate than what you originally described for the Activity Center.

What I recommend

Don't patch this file. Replace its data layer entirely.

Keep the useful structural ideas:

activity-blog.js
├── configuration
├── formatting helpers
├── Markdown parsing
├── normalization
├── sorting
├── fetching
├── DOM rendering
├── controller
└── initialization/export

But make the actual data pipeline:

blog-index.json
      ↓
fetch paths
      ↓
fetch each post.md
      ↓
parse front matter
      ↓
normalize:
{
    title,
    date,
    description,
    cover,
    url
}
      ↓
sort newest → oldest
      ↓
take MAX_POSTS
      ↓
render

And that's it.

One other thing: you named this activity-blog.js, whereas our original module list had activity-blogs.js. I'd actually stick with activity-blog.js now because the module represents the blog activity feed as a singular subsystem. Just update the planned JS tree accordingly.
